Returns the string representation of the input value.

A versatile polymorphic function defined for different input types, `to_str` uses corresponding converter functions from [`str`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/string#type::std::str) to specific types via the format argument _fmt_.

When converting [`bytes`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/bytes#type::std::bytes), [`datetime`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/datetime#type::std::datetime), [`cal::local_datetime`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/datetime#type::cal::local_datetime), [`cal::local_date`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/datetime#type::cal::local_date), [`cal::local_time`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/datetime#type::cal::local_time), [`duration`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/datetime#type::std::duration) this function is the inverse of [`to_bytes()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/bytes#function::std::to_bytes) (added in 4.0), [`to_datetime()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/datetime#function::std::to_datetime), [`cal::to_local_datetime()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/datetime#function::cal::to_local_datetime), [`cal::to_local_date()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/datetime#function::cal::to_local_date), [`cal::to_local_time()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/datetime#function::cal::to_local_time), [`to_duration()`](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/datetime#function::std::to_duration), correspondingly.

For valid date and time formatting patterns see [here](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/stdlib/string#ref-std-converters-datetime-fmt).

Copy

